        <section class="city-guide">
          <h2>The old town and its gates</h2>
          <p>The restricted zone covers Bari Vecchia between the Norman-Swabian castle and the waterfront at Santa Chiara, behind seven camera gates. The rule is the simplest and strictest kind: <strong>permit holders only, twenty-four hours a day, every day of the year</strong>. There is no window in which the old town opens to ordinary traffic, and no time of day at which driving in becomes acceptable.</p>
          <p>Permits are for residents and the domiciled — one per household, one vehicle. <strong>There is no tourist or visitor permit</strong>, and no advance-booking scheme of the kind some other Italian towns run. The only temporary authorisation is for disabled-badge vehicles, arranged by telephone with the Polizia Municipale for a single entry.</p>
          <p>The arrangement has been reworked more than once in recent years, most recently in mid-2026 with a further gate switched on and interior parking near the waterfront restricted, so older advice about which streets and car parks you can use is not reliable.</p>
          <p>Parts of the old town go further than a ZTL: the area around the Basilica di San Nicola is a pedestrian zone closed to vehicles at all hours, not merely restricted, and some gates open only in narrow windows during the day for loading. None of this is a problem if you have no intention of driving in — which is the right intention, since the lanes are barely wide enough for a small car and there is nowhere to leave one.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="city-guide">
          <h2>Where to leave the car</h2>
          <p>The Pane e Pomodoro car park, on the seafront promenade south of the old town, is the easiest option for a visit: a large surface lot at a modest hourly rate with a daily cap, an easy walk from the cathedral and the entrance to Bari Vecchia. The Mercato del Pesce lot near the harbour is the same idea on the other side.</p>
          <p>In the Murat grid, street parking is metered and dearer by the hour, while the strip along the seafront beside the old town is cheaper. The city also runs park-and-ride sites on the approaches with bus links in, which make sense if you are leaving the car for several days.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="city-guide">
          <h2>If you are arriving for a ferry</h2>
          <p>Bari is one of Italy’s main passenger ports, with sailings to Greece, Albania, Montenegro and Croatia, and a great many people pass through with a car and a deadline. The port has its own access arrangements and approach roads, and it sits immediately alongside the old town — exactly the situation in which a driver running late and following a phone ends up in the zone. Follow the port signs rather than the shortest route to the terminal, and allow more time than the distance suggests.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="city-guide">
          <h2>Staying inside the old town</h2>
          <p>Bari Vecchia has a growing number of B&amp;Bs and short-let apartments, and this is where Bari differs sharply from most Italian towns: <strong>the ordinance provides no guest-access route at all</strong>. Elsewhere a hotel registers your plate and you drive in; here there is no such mechanism. A property can only help if it holds a <em>passo carrabile</em> permit tied to an off-street space of its own, which few addresses in the old town have.</p>
          <p>So assume you are arriving on foot with your luggage. Ask before booking how close a car can actually get and where you are expected to leave it, and treat any confident promise of driving to the door with suspicion — many addresses in Bari Vecchia cannot be reached by car under any permission.</p>
        </section>

